              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_4_image_5.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.247,
            0.405,
            0.685,
            0.524
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "parts — "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Carpels",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " (female) and "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "stamens",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " (male). Stamens burst and release yellow dust like "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "pollen grains",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ". Wind, water, insects, bats, and birds help carry pollen from the stamens to the carpels of the same and/or different flowers. This process is called "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "pollination",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " (Fig. 12.2). It is essential for the formation of fruits and seeds."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
